-- Passage rg12-word-parts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('451f4af7-ccaf-5ea7-9a85-5e91edabce6e', 'rg12-word-parts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Painted Worlds in the Round$q$, $q$Long before films or virtual reality, city dwellers in the nineteenth century paid admission to step inside a painting. These attractions, called panoramas, were enormous canvases stretched around the inside wall of a round building known as a rotunda. A single painting might run more than a hundred yards around the room's circumference and rise as tall as a four-story house.

Visitors entered through a dim corridor and climbed a staircase to a central platform. From there, they could turn in every direction and see one continuous scene: a harbor crowded with ships, a mountain range at sunrise, or a busy city square. Painters hid the top and bottom edges of the canvas with a canopy and with real objects arranged in the foreground, such as barrels, fences, and sand, so that the line between object and painting blurred.

The illusion depended on careful planning. Artists projected their sketches onto the canvas in sections, and teams of assistants then filled in the sky, the buildings, and countless tiny figures. Daylight entered through hidden windows above, so the scene appeared to glow with natural light.

Crowds congregated at popular panoramas for hours, pointing out details to one another and returning with friends. Standing on the platform gave many visitors an almost omniscient feeling, as if they could know everything happening in a place at once.

By the early twentieth century, motion pictures had drawn audiences away, and most rotundas were torn down or converted to other uses. A few panoramas survive today, carefully restored, and their visitors still drift slowly around the platform, trying to find the edge of the painted world.$q$, 'draft')
